<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shah Rukh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1409">1409</a>–<a href="/years/1447">1447</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shah Rukh was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> from 1409 to 1447, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. His reign brought relative stability and a flourishing of Persian art and literature. Accounts of the Timurid Empire treat his long rule as a period of consolidation before the empire's fragmentation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-rukh.jpg" alt="Shah Rukh" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1435-1436 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Shah_Rukh,_painted_in_his_lifetime_in_1435-36,_by_commission_of_his_son_Ibrahim_Sultan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shah Rukh was born on 20 August 1377, the youngest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s four sons. Some sources name his mother as Saray Mulk Khanum, a Chaghatai princess and Timur's chief consort, while the historian Khwandamir names a woman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a>. He was raised by Saray Mulk alongside Timur's grandson Khalil Sultan. In 1397 Timur appointed him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> as his capital, and he was never promoted beyond that post in his father's lifetime. Sources give no explanation; suggestions include his mother's status, his reputation for modesty and piety and his rejection of the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. In 1402 he commanded the left wing at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> died in 1405 without a clearly appointed heir. Khalil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> proclaimed himself emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and seized the treasury and Samarqand, and the succession war lasted years. Support among his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> drained away from Khalil Sultan, and Shah Rukh took Samarqand unopposed. Khalil Sultan submitted and died in 1411. Shah Rukh held court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, not Samarqand, and made his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>.</p><p>He recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> in 1414 and Kirman two years later. In the west the Qara Qoyunlu held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and Shah Rukh led three campaigns against them, in 1420, 1429 and 1434. The last brought stability for the rest of his reign under the Turkoman ruler Jahan Shah. He repeatedly put down rebellions by relatives. In 1426 an attacker, Ahmed Lur, stabbed him outside a mosque; he recovered, and an investigation linked Lur to the Hurufis, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> sect, leading to purges that reached intellectuals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>.</p><p>He ruled as an Islamic sultan rather than a Turco-Mongol conqueror. In 1411 he abolished the Mongol yarghu tribunals in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> courts and took the titles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> and Padshah. His empress Gawhar Shad built the Gawhar Shad Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> and the Gawhar Shad Mosque and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. Embassies passed between Herat and the Ming court in 1414-1420, and Shah Rukh asserted his independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shah Rukh died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a> on 13 March 1447, after putting down the revolt of his grandson Sultan Muhammad. Chaos broke out in the camp, and his grandson Abdal-Latif took the body and Gawhar Shad hostage before another grandson, Ala al-Dawla, freed them and buried him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. Ulugh Beg later had the body reburied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s in the Gur-e-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> at Samarqand. Struggles among his descendants followed, and after civil wars control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> passed out of his line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shah Rukh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Qara Yusuf's death in 1420 <b>Shah Rukh</b> beat Qara Iskander in 1420–21 and briefly occupied Tabriz, beat him again in 1429, and in a third expedition of 1434–35 installed Iskander's brother Jahan Shah as vassal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors, above all <b>Shah Rukh</b> (r. 1405–1447, sole ruler from 1409) and his son Ulugh Beg (governor of Samarkand from 1409 and briefly ruler, 1447–1449), invested heavily in rebuilding agriculture, irrigation, urban infrastructure and commerce in the Transoxianan and Khorasanian heartlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Timur conquered Mazandaran in 1392 and deported the family, but <b>Shah Rukh</b> restored them.</blockquote><a href="/state/marashi-dynasty">Mar'ashi dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in Ray, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.66,66.98&amp;year=1428" aria-label="Samarkand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6202 744 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shah Rukh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6652 1014h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6652 1014h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.66,66.98&amp;year=1428">What was here in 1428? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
